```java
final class Money {
    private final long cents;
    private final String currency;

    Money(long cents, String currency) {
        this.cents = cents;
        this.currency = currency;
    }

    long getCents() { return cents; }
    String getCurrency() { return currency; }

    Money plus(Money other) {
        // returns a NEW Money, never mutates this or other
        return new Money(this.cents + other.cents, this.currency);
    }
}
```

This is Java, but the checklist generalizes to any language: mark the class itself non-extendable (`final`), mark every field final/read-only, assign every field only in the constructor, and expose no method that mutates state -- `plus()` returns a new instance instead of changing `this`.

Missing any one of these reopens a hole: a mutable subclass could add a setter, or a field left non-final could be reassigned later.
